  "Question": "The radius of a cylindrical box is \\(8\\) inches and the height is \\(3\\) inches. The number of inches that may be added to either the radius or the height to give the same nonzero increase in volume is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(1\\)",
    "B": "\\(5\\dfrac13\\)",
    "C": "Any number",
    "D": "Non-existent",
    "E": "None of these"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "The original volume is the same in both comparisons, so we can calculate just the added volume. Let \\(t>0\\) be the number of inches added to either dimension. Using the cylinder formula \\(V=\\pi r^2h\\), increasing the radius adds\n\\[\n3\\pi\\bigl((8+t)^2-8^2\\bigr)=\\pi t(48+3t)\n\\]\ncubic inches. Increasing the height adds \\(\\pi\\cdot8^2t=64\\pi t\\) cubic inches.\n\nFor the increases to be equal,\n\\[\n\\pi t(48+3t)=64\\pi t.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\pi t>0\\), division by this common factor is valid. Thus\n\\[\n48+3t=64,\n\\qquad t=\\frac{16}{3}=5\\frac13.\n\\]\nThis positive value gives a nonzero increase in both cases. The answer is \\(\\boxed{5\\frac13}\\) inches, choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\).",
